            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies symbolic 'elders' (especially the twenty-four in Revelation) as the representative leadership of the redeemed people of God (Israel and the Church) enthroned in heaven. A prominent alternative view identifies them as angelic beings comprising God's divine council.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book identifies elders as the leaders of the assemblies who are seated in the heavenly court, explicitly characterizing them as part of the host of heaven and as the brightest of the stars.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Representative leaders of God's covenant people in the heavenly throne room.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Leaders of the assemblies seated in the heavenly court, characterized as stars and the heavenly host.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"While the consensus views the elders either as redeemed humanity or as an angelic divine council (treating these as distinct options), the book merges these concepts by placing human 'leaders of the assemblies' among the 'host of heaven' and identifying them with astral imagery ('stars').\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book affirms the core identity of human representative leadership ('leaders of the assemblies') but expands their symbolic identity to include cosmic and heavenly council imagery.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book matches the primary consensus by defining elders as 'leaders of the assemblies' (supported by Matthew 19:28), but refines the symbol by explicitly integrating these leaders into the 'host of heaven' and equating them with 'stars' (supported by 1 Kings 22, Isaiah 24, and Psalm 89). This effectively blends the consensus's primary view with its major alternative.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The consensus generally treats redeemed human leaders and the angelic host/divine council as mutually exclusive interpretations of the twenty-four elders, whereas the book applies the titles of the heavenly host and stars directly to the leaders of the assemblies.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
